<UserControl xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
             xmlns:vm="using:Winnow.App.ViewModels"
             xmlns:views="using:Winnow.App.Views"
             x:Class="Winnow.App.Views.FeedView"
             x:DataType="vm:FeedViewModel">

    <!--
        ══ The Feed (M8) ════════════════════════════════════════════════════
        The screen the app opens on, and the point of the product: five sections
        over one scoring pass, each section stating its own reason and each card
        stating its own.

        ══ THE RAILS ARE GONE, AND THEY WERE WRONG ══════════════════════════
        This screen used to run each section as a horizontal rail. The argument
        for that was "a heading, a pitch and a row of reasons on one scanline",
        and it does not survive contact with a desktop. A rail is a television
        convention: it assumes a remote control and a viewer who cannot see the
        pointer. Here the wheel does the wrong thing by default, the shelf never
        says how much it holds, and everything past the right edge is
        functionally invisible — the old note in this file boasted that a rail
        shows "how much is off to the right", which is an admission that the
        content was hidden. A recommendation nobody scrolls to was not made.

        So every section is a GRID BOUND TO THE WINDOW. Cards wrap to as many
        per row as the width fits, the page scrolls vertically, and nothing on
        this screen scrolls horizontally anywhere. A section of ten is two or
        three rows, all of them visible. Views/FeedGrid.cs carries the geometry.

        ══ AND THE SECTIONS ARE NOW ISLANDS, WHICH IS NOT DECORATION ════════
        Stacking sideways-scrolling rails delineated them by the act of
        scrolling: you knew where one shelf ended because the other one did not
        move with it. Stacked grids of similar-looking cards have no such
        boundary, and a heading with a wider margin over it is not one either —
        someone scrolling past would have to read the nearest heading to know
        which claim the card under their pointer belongs to.

        So a section is bounded on all four sides: a 1px Line box at RadiusPane,
        with its own header divided from its cards by a rule that crosses it.
        That is the LIBRARY PANE'S OWN SHAPE at section scale — an island whose
        first band is a header and whose field is below the rule (§15.1, §11.1)
        — so the screen borrows a structure the window already teaches rather
        than inventing a second one. It costs no ink and no fill: the box is an
        outline, the ground inside it is the pane's own, and the transparency
        slider reaches the desktop between the cards exactly as before.

        The hierarchy is stated in the two line weights. The ENCLOSURE takes
        Line and the CARDS take LineSoft, so the structure outranks its contents
        rather than tying with them.

        WHY THERE IS NO VIRTUALIZATION HERE, and it is measured rather than
        assumed. The engine caps a section at ten items and omits empty ones, so
        the ceiling is fifty cards — against the six hundred that made CoverWall
        necessary. CoverWall is also the wrong tool twice over: it is uniform,
        and these cards are not (a row's height is set by its longest reason).

        WHAT THE PATCHED SECTION DOES NOT GET. It is the app's headline story
        and therefore the most tempting place in the interface to spend Flare —
        on its heading, on its box, on its count. It gets none of it. Every
        section is drawn identically and the patched one leads by being first,
        which is the engine's claim order. Flare's one appearance on this screen
        is the unread dot on a cover, which is what it has always meant (§2).
    -->

    <UserControl.Styles>

        <!-- ══ A section ════════════════════════════════════════════════════
             The enclosure, and the reason someone scrolling past always knows
             which claim they are inside. Outline only — a fill would either be
             a second opaque plate the size of the pane (the transparency
             slider's top end pays for that) or, as a translucent veil of
             SurfaceRaised over a translucent ground, the inverted elevation
             §14.2 spells out. Line and a radius say it for nothing. -->
        <Style Selector="Border.section">
            <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{StaticResource Line}"/>
            <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1"/>
            <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{StaticResource RadiusPane}"/>
        </Style>

        <!-- The section heading. Bricolage, because a section title is the
             app's own voice making a claim — but not display-l, which is the
             screen's own name and has to outrank it. -->
        <Style Selector="TextBlock.shelf">
            <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource DisplayFont}"/>
            <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="Bold"/>
            <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="17"/>
            <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource Text}"/>
        </Style>

    </UserControl.Styles>

    <Grid RowDefinitions="Auto,*" Background="{StaticResource PaneGround}">

        <!-- ══ Header ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
             Same construction as the Stores screen's: the name, one line of
             what this is, and a rule under it. The rule is FULL BLEED where a
             section's is inset inside a box, which is the whole of how the
             screen's own header is told apart from the five under it.

             The count is a NUMBER, so it sets in Plex Mono with tabular figures
             like every number in the app — and it is only stated once there is
             one to state, because "0 games scored" while the pass is out would
             be a lie the screen tells for half a second. -->
        <Border Grid.Row="0"
                Padding="24,20,24,18"
                BorderBrush="{StaticResource Line}"
                BorderThickness="0,0,0,1">
            <Grid ColumnDefinitions="*,Auto">
                <StackPanel Grid.Column="0" Spacing="7">
                    <TextBlock Classes="display-l" Text="{Binding Title}"/>

                    <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="6"
                                IsVisible="{Binding ShowCandidates}">
                        <TextBlock Classes="data"
                                   Foreground="{StaticResource Text}"
                                   Text="{Binding CandidateCountText}"/>
                        <TextBlock Classes="body"
                                   Foreground="{StaticResource TextDim}"
                                   Text="games scored today, and every card says why it's on it."/>
                    </StackPanel>

                    <!-- §6's confidence copy, driven by the tier the engine
                         reports. It goes quiet once the library has enough
                         history that saying anything would be an apology for
                         nothing. -->
                    <TextBlock Classes="body" FontSize="12"
                               MaxWidth="640"
                               HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                               TextWrapping="Wrap"
                               LineHeight="18"
                               Foreground="{StaticResource TextDim}"
                               IsVisible="{Binding HasConfidenceNote}"
                               Text="{Binding ConfidenceNote}"/>
                </StackPanel>

                <!-- ══ The way into "what you've told the feed" ════════════
                     It hangs off THIS header rather than sitting in the rail's
                     SETTINGS section, and the argument is three-part. It is not
                     a preference — it configures nothing; it is the record of
                     acts performed on this screen, and filing an audit trail
                     under app settings files it as a knob. Its value is a number
                     that grows, and that section's own note forbids counts on
                     its rows ("a settings row would only ever say a constant").
                     And §12.2's rule — the rail never leaves the user on a screen
                     their click did not describe — cuts against a SETTINGS row
                     that lands them inside the Feed.

                     So it is one click from the cards it is about, beside the
                     count of what those cards were drawn from, and it is the
                     second route back from a dismissal: the card's own receipt
                     is the first, and it is the one that catches a misclick.

                     The count is Plex Mono with tabular figures like every other
                     number, and it is stated only once there is one. -->
                <Button Grid.Column="1"
                        Classes="act quiet"
                        Padding="12,6"
                        FontSize="12"
                        VerticalAlignment="Top"
                        Command="{Binding ToggleHistoryCommand}">
                    <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="8">
                        <TextBlock VerticalAlignment="Center" Text="{Binding HistoryLabel}"/>
                        <TextBlock Classes="data-s"
                                   VerticalAlignment="Center"
                                   Foreground="{StaticResource TextDim}"
                                   IsVisible="{Binding ShowHistoryCount}"
                                   Text="{Binding History.CountText}"/>
                    </StackPanel>
                </Button>
            </Grid>
        </Border>

        <!-- ══ Working / quiet / broken ═════════════════════════════════════
             One sentence, never a blank pane and never a spinner. All three
             states point at the rail, because the library is one click away and
             a feed that cannot answer must not read as an app that cannot. -->
        <Border Grid.Row="1"
                Padding="32"
                IsVisible="{Binding ShowMessage}">
            <StackPanel MaxWidth="480" Spacing="18"
                        HorizontalAlignment="Center" VerticalAlignment="Center">
                <TextBlock Classes="body-l"
                           TextWrapping="Wrap"
                           TextAlignment="Center"
                           LineHeight="22"
                           Foreground="{StaticResource TextDim}"
                           Text="{Binding Message}"/>
                <!-- Only a FAILED pass offers this. A quiet feed is not
                     something to try again at, and a button saying so would be
                     the app arguing with its own answer. -->
                <Button Classes="act quiet"
                        Content="Try again"
                        HorizontalAlignment="Center"
                        IsVisible="{Binding CanRetry}"
                        Command="{Binding LoadCommand}"/>
            </StackPanel>
        </Border>

        <!-- ══ The sections ═════════════════════════════════════════════════
             The ONE scroller on this screen, and it is vertical. Its right edge
             is the window's, so its scrollbar takes §9.1's edge inset from the
             window's own ScrollBar:vertical style; there is no longer any
             interior scroller to opt back out of it. -->
        <ScrollViewer Grid.Row="1"
                      Name="Page"
                      IsVisible="{Binding ShowShelves}"
                      HorizontalScrollBarVisibility="Disabled"
                      VerticalScrollBarVisibility="Auto">
            <ItemsControl ItemsSource="{Binding Shelves}" Margin="24,24,24,32">

                <!-- 24 between islands, against the 14 gutter inside one: a
                     step on §4's scale rather than a nudge, so the spacing is
                     pulling the same direction the boxes are. Anything closer
                     to the gutter and the two gaps read as the same gap, which
                     is the spacing arguing with the delineation. -->
                <ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
                    <ItemsPanelTemplate>
                        <StackPanel Spacing="24"/>
                    </ItemsPanelTemplate>
                </ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>

                <ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
                    <DataTemplate x:DataType="vm:FeedShelfViewModel">
                        <Border Classes="section">
                            <StackPanel>

                                <!-- The section's header band. -->
                                <StackPanel Margin="18,15,18,14" Spacing="3">
                                    <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="10">
                                        <TextBlock Classes="shelf" Text="{Binding Title}"/>

                                        <!-- How many are in the section, beside
                                             the name rather than out at the
                                             box's far edge: a count belongs to
                                             the thing it counts.

                                             It says something different now
                                             that the whole section is visible.
                                             On a rail it told you how much was
                                             hidden; here it is the section
                                             agreeing with what you can already
                                             count, which is what makes a short
                                             section read as finished rather
                                             than as half-loaded. -->
                                        <TextBlock Classes="data-s"
                                                   VerticalAlignment="Center"
                                                   Text="{Binding CountText}"/>
                                    </StackPanel>

                                    <!-- The section's own pitch, in the engine's
                                         words. It is the sentence the cards
                                         under it are all instances of. -->
                                    <TextBlock Classes="body"
                                               MaxWidth="720"
                                               HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                                               TextWrapping="Wrap"
                                               LineHeight="19"
                                               Foreground="{StaticResource TextDim}"
                                               Text="{Binding Blurb}"/>
                                </StackPanel>

                                <!-- The rule that crosses the box, exactly as
                                     the command bar's crosses the library pane.
                                     Above it the section says what it is;
                                     below it are the games. -->
                                <Border Height="1" Background="{StaticResource Line}"/>

                                <ItemsControl ItemsSource="{Binding Cards}" Margin="16">
                                    <ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
                                        <ItemsPanelTemplate>
                                            <!-- Window-bound: the column count
                                                 falls out of the width, the row
                                                 divides it evenly, and no card
                                                 is charged for a gutter it does
                                                 not have a neighbour on. -->
                                            <views:FeedGrid MinItemWidth="420" Gutter="14"/>
                                        </ItemsPanelTemplate>
                                    </ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
                                    <ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
                                        <DataTemplate x:DataType="vm:FeedCardViewModel">
                                            <views:FeedCardView/>
                                        </DataTemplate>
                                    </ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
                                </ItemsControl>

                            </StackPanel>
                        </Border>
                    </DataTemplate>
                </ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
            </ItemsControl>
        </ScrollViewer>

        <!-- ══ What you've told the feed ═══════════════════════
             The inspection surface, and it takes the body rather than opening a
             popup. §12.3's reason, unchanged: Avalonia's focus adorner does not
             render inside a popup (a popup is its own root and has no adorner
             layer), so every ring in a flyout here would have to be hand-drawn,
             while in the window's own tree §8's ring and a linear tab order both
             come free. Escape closes it, from anywhere on the screen.

             It borrows the section island's shape — a 1px Line box at
             RadiusPane, a header band, a rule across it, rows below — because
             that is the structure this screen already teaches, and a second one
             would be a second thing to learn on a surface whose whole job is to
             be legible.

             EVERY ROW IS SHOWN, including the ones that no longer bind. A
             verdict the user took back is two facts (they said it; they undid
             it) and both are on the row; a snooze that lapsed left no write
             anywhere, so its row is the only evidence it ever happened, and "why
             did this come back" is a question this screen has to be able to
             answer. Hiding either would make the loop exactly the black box the
             charter forbids. -->
        <ScrollViewer Grid.Row="1"
                      IsVisible="{Binding ShowHistory}"
                      HorizontalScrollBarVisibility="Disabled"
                      VerticalScrollBarVisibility="Auto">
            <Border Classes="section" Margin="24,24,24,32">
                <StackPanel>

                    <StackPanel Margin="18,15,18,14" Spacing="3">
                        <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="10">
                            <TextBlock Classes="shelf" Text="{Binding History.Title}"/>
                            <TextBlock Classes="data-s"
                                       VerticalAlignment="Center"
                                       IsVisible="{Binding History.HasEntries}"
                                       Text="{Binding History.CountText}"/>
                        </StackPanel>
                        <TextBlock Classes="body"
                                   MaxWidth="720"
                                   HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                                   TextWrapping="Wrap"
                                   LineHeight="19"
                                   Foreground="{StaticResource TextDim}"
                                   Text="Everything you've said to a card, newest first — including the ones you took back and the ones that ran out on their own. Nothing here is deleted."/>
                    </StackPanel>

                    <Border Height="1" Background="{StaticResource Line}"/>

                    <!-- Empty is a direction, not a mood (§7): it says where the
                         controls are, because somebody opening this before ever
                         using them has asked a reasonable question. -->
                    <TextBlock Classes="body"
                               Margin="18,16,18,18"
                               MaxWidth="620"
                               HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                               TextWrapping="Wrap"
                               LineHeight="19"
                               Foreground="{StaticResource TextDim}"
                               IsVisible="{Binding History.ShowMessage}"
                               Text="{Binding History.Message}"/>

                    <ItemsControl ItemsSource="{Binding History.Entries}"
                                  IsVisible="{Binding History.HasEntries}">
                        <ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
                            <DataTemplate x:DataType="vm:FeedHistoryEntryViewModel">
                                <!-- A row, ruled off from the next. 44px is the
                                     list view's row height and this is the same
                                     kind of object — a record you scan rather
                                     than a card you choose from. -->
                                <Border BorderBrush="{StaticResource LineSoft}"
                                        BorderThickness="0,0,0,1"
                                        Padding="18,12">
                                    <Grid ColumnDefinitions="*,Auto" ColumnSpacing="14">
                                        <StackPanel Grid.Column="0" Spacing="4">
                                            <TextBlock Text="{Binding Title}"
                                                       FontFamily="{StaticResource DisplayFont}"
                                                       FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="14"
                                                       TextWrapping="Wrap"
                                                       Foreground="{StaticResource Text}"/>

                                            <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="8">
                                                <!-- Which of the two things they
                                                     said, in words. Never a
                                                     colour: the two kinds are
                                                     told apart by their names
                                                     for §12.1's reason — a mark
                                                     survives only while there is
                                                     one of them, and this app's
                                                     one mark is the unread
                                                     dot. -->
                                                <TextBlock Classes="label"
                                                           VerticalAlignment="Center"
                                                           Foreground="{StaticResource TextDim}"
                                                           Text="{Binding KindLabel}"/>
                                                <TextBlock Classes="body"
                                                           VerticalAlignment="Center"
                                                           Foreground="{StaticResource TextDim}"
                                                           Text="{Binding StatusNote}"/>
                                                <TextBlock Classes="data"
                                                           VerticalAlignment="Center"
                                                           Foreground="{StaticResource TextDim}"
                                                           IsVisible="{Binding HasStatusDate}"
                                                           Text="{Binding StatusDate}"/>
                                            </StackPanel>
                                        </StackPanel>

                                        <!-- Only a standing verdict offers one.
                                             An undone row already has been and a
                                             lapsed snooze undid itself — a button
                                             there would invite an act that does
                                             nothing. -->
                                        <Button Grid.Column="1"
                                                Classes="act quiet"
                                                FontSize="12"
                                                Padding="14,6"
                                                VerticalAlignment="Center"
                                                Content="Undo"
                                                IsVisible="{Binding CanUndo}"
                                                Command="{Binding UndoCommand}"/>
                                    </Grid>
                                </Border>
                            </DataTemplate>
                        </ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
                    </ItemsControl>

                </StackPanel>
            </Border>
        </ScrollViewer>

    </Grid>

</UserControl>
